Z||000000||fierospeeder||10-18-2000||03:19 AM||||I always wanted a truck, almost bought one, but got my tbird instead.  But im getting the desire to get one again.  I keep on seeing the old chevy trucks at the junkyards and it makes me want one.<P>I want one of those 87 or earlier chevy silverados or those k10s?  i dont really know the names of the chevy trucksk10=k30s.<BR>I seen one, at the junkyard, which was a diseal but it had a cab with four doors i believe and it was a carb engine.  <P>Is anyone into trucks, and what are some disadvantages of them, besides gas guzzeling, slower and big in size, and what is it like driving one.<P>I want to get one with a carb, v8, it does suck dealing with fuel injection problems.  I think some of them came with 2 gas tanks?  I would like to get a cab, but will that be a overkill and would it make it too long.  I do want the 4X4 option.  I know the term 1/2, 3/4, 1 ton comes up, what does it mean.  I will be putting a 3 inch body lift <BR>plus suspension lift, eventually.  If anyone has websites of these trucks that would be great.  ||||reg||1
Z||000001||RossT||10-18-2000||03:39 AM||||I have a 1985 full size half ton (k10) chevy<BR>4X4.  I bought it from my borther in law about 6 years ago. It currently has 75,000 miles on it. Its got the 305 not the 350. <P>good things:  the 305 is really good on gas.<BR>I am getting about 20 on the highway (with overdrive) and around 12-15 intown. The truck runs very well.  Great in the winter.<BR>I have towed 2 fieros home with it!!!  Easy to work on, and parts galore in stock at your local parts place. Brand new crate engines are cheap.  Insurance is cheap.<P>bad things: prone to rust, you got to keep the salt of it in the winter.  Vacuum hoses EVERYWHERE.  and I mean everywhere.  There are hoses that I don't have a clue as to what they do.  The 305 is a dog. <P><BR>This is my first truck, and I will nevery be without one again.  I love it!!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||10-18-2000||05:26 AM||||I thought the K series was for GMC and the C series was for Chevy.<P>fierospeeder--the 10, 20, & 30 relate to the loading capacity.  "10" is the 1/2 ton, "20" is the 3/4 ton (called a heavy half ton by some manufacturers), and the "30" is the one ton.  If you want to tow anything of any significance, get a diesel.  <P>If you don't want a gas guzzling, slow big truck, don't put big tires on it.  That'll kill your gas mileage and performance.  One of the disadvantages with any older truck is the problem with rear wheel lockup.  Most of the newer trucks have rear wheel ABS (for what it's worth), but I doubt you'll find that in anything as old as an '87.<P>If you want 4 wheel drive and plan on putting a lift kit/bigger tires on your truck, don't get a 1 ton.  Those are usually the duallys.  (It's possible to get a 1 ton with single rear wheels, but they're tough to find.)  Unless you plan on hauling some serious weight (not likely with a lift kit), go with a 1/2 ton.  ||||reg||1
Z||000003||RossT||10-18-2000||05:41 AM||||MM,  the "c" is for 2wd, the "k" is for 4x4.||||reg||1
