Z||000006||loafer87gt||10-18-2000||12:13 PM||||I bought a 88 Toyota 1/4 ton, but the old guy who had it before me had swapped in on of the overhead cam landcruiser 3.4 engines. I have never had a problem with it, and a 190 HP in a little truck that makes it quite the screamer! I love having a truck and a car, I hate driving my car on gravel roads (quite a bit in Saskatchewan!), and for hauling and moving around stuff, well, its a lot better than my Fiero. I've never owned a 4x4 though, probably a good thing as I end up getting myself in enough trouble with 2wd. The nicest truck i even ridden in was my buddies old 1991 Dodge Dakota 318 magnum 4x4. It was an extremely "tight" feeling truck, and with a new computer board and exhaust it had a whole $hitload of power!||||reg||1
Z||000007||2xsess||10-18-2000||12:31 PM||||I had a pickup and I've driven both my dads 90 Chevy Stepsides, one had the 4.3 with a 5spd and the other was the 350 auto. the v8 had alot of power and it would haul ass but the rearends are light and they like to get a little loose on wet roads so keep that in mind too. I'll never have another pick-up without the extended cab either.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>85GT||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||10-18-2000||05:05 PM||||I had and have lots of trucks, mostly Chevys and many were 4X4. I recommend getting a Chevy 3/4 ton if you're getting a four wheel drive. They have a much heavier duty drive train compared to the 1/2 tonners and hold up better. Also, the higher the tonnage rating, the more it will safely tow or haul. A one ton is overkill unless you are really doing heavy hauling (like a monster RV). Most older 4X4 trucks can be a bit bouncy on the road without a load. Especially with a lift kit and big tires. Basically, they are a little light in the ass-end when running empty. I like putting a few sand bags in the back for winter driving. Big tires and lift kits also will throw your handling off a bit, of course it never will corner like a Fiero no matter what you do. The older Chevy trucks are study, dependable trucks, but are prone to body rust, like was mentioned. Don't get a diesel if you live in Chicago and want to start the truck up on sub-zero mornings. If you need a heavy hauler, get a 454 gas motor in your truck, but forget about gas mileage. Personally, the ol' 350 small block can't be beat for a good multi-purpose power plant. My 1986 Suburban  (really a truck in disguise) with a tired ol' carbed 350 gets only about 12 to 14 mpg on the highway with a 2.5 inch lift kit and big tires. Yes, many trucks have optional dual gas tanks. If we haven't answered all your questions, then keep asking.<p>[This message has been edited by batboy (edited 10-18-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||Raydar||10-18-2000||06:05 PM||||Do S-10s and Sonomas count?<BR>I've got a 92 Sonoma extended cab with a 4.3 and 5 speed. <BR>The only thing that I haven't been able to do with it was tow a car-hauler. (Trailer, not dolly.) Had to borrow my father-in-law's F-150 to bring home my latest project. I know another guy who tows Fieros on dollies all the time with an S-10.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>Out of my mind.<BR>Back in five minutes.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Cooter||10-20-2000||10:06 PM||||My 3/4 ton Suburban is a pretty cool truck. It will 9 people, their luggage and pull a loaded enclosed car hauler without any trouble. Of course the mileage is not that great, 12-18 MPG, but since it is not a daily driver I am not too worried.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Songman||10-20-2000||10:46 PM||||I love my trucks! I have an almost restored 69 Ford F-100 shortbed with a 390 and C6. If anyone knows where a decent front clip is for a good price let me know...<P>But my toy truck is a 79 Dodge Power Wagon W15 stepside raised up 3 inches and sitting on 35s. I love this truck! And it has pulled every Jeep and truck that all of my friends drive out of the muck! They go spend all that money on special axles and hubs and all that crap and my old ugly brown Dodge just keeps on showing them what 4x4 really means! And it never costs me anything!||||reg||10
Z||000012||86SE||10-21-2000||02:12 AM||||I have a 1974 Ford F-100 longbed pickup with the 390 V8 and the 3-on-the-tree transmission.  Its only 2wd but it can go through a lot of mud.<P>I bought it for $100 bucks.  Interior is in extremely good condition, body has a little rust, paint is faded, engine burns a little oil, and it sucks away a lot of gas.  I only get about 100 yards of burnouts for evry 2 gallons or so.  This truck has a ton of power so burnouts are easy, and kind of fun when you wind up the big block to 4K, drop the clutch and slam the throttle to the floor.  When the 390 with the exhaust cut off underneath the passenger compartment (no muffler) roars up to about 5.5-6K LOOK OUT cuz it starts to cackel and it roars louder then the tire screeching on the pavement.  Its cool!<P>I have already gotten my $100 worth of fun out of it..........||||reg||14
Z||000013||Philphine||10-21-2000||06:05 PM||||got the 2nd most ragged (and thats giving it the benifit of the dought) s10 blazer on the road. my 1st truck. got it for a beater and winter car 5 years ago. never been in the shop, i've been able to fix what's gone wrong with it so far. i like it enough that i want to get a nicer one in the future.the small size makes it appealing but if i got something else truckwise it would probaly be a suburban or big pickup. dreamwise i'd want a typhoon or a v-8 converted blazer.||||reg||1
