Z||000015||fierospeeder||10-23-2000||09:27 PM||||I just got a truck trader magazine, so i can get a look at the choices.  It was hard looking up homepages with altavista, but i found a great site with yahoo, on 79-87 chevy/gmc trucks. <A HREF="http://www.chuckschevytruckpages.com" TARGET=_blank>www.chuckschevytruckpages.com</A> <P>it doesn't seem like there are many earlier models being sold, more of the 90s and later??<P>I have decided to get an earlier one, 87-83, cause they had the straight axle in the front.  Also i want the carbed engine, because i want to try something without fuel injection, too many problems.  And parts are a lot cheaper.  im going to look for that 3/4 ton truck 4X4.  maybe a 305 or that 350 engine.  The silverado name comes a lot so im going to get that one, unless i see a gmc.<P>Im still "iffy" about the extended cab.  They had those four door pickups, but a concern is, will it be a very long truck, and does anyone have any approx. lengths?  Someone mentioned that the ext cab is a good idea.   What would the pros and cons be with a long wheelbase, but less of a manuevability?  I know i will love the extra room, it will help keeping my stuff protected by the rain.  Was the four door extended cab an option with the 3/4 ton during the 80s, so far i have seen them on the duals and diseals.<P>Also, can anyone describe the feeling of driving a truck when the suspension is raised 3-6 inches (and or a body lift), with the 30 inch tires that can crush a SUV?<BR>Someone mentioned that a couple of sandbags would be good in the snow.  How bad will it be in snow, with the tail sliding, worse then my tbird??   <P>By the way, Sams club, has 40lb bags of oil pickup(kitty litter) for about $3.50.  Which wouldn't be to bad of a buy.<P>thanks you guys <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000016||batboy||10-23-2000||10:41 PM||||1) Keep looking, maybe try autotrader.com, there is lots of those old Chevy 4X4's around.<P>2) The Silverado is the fanciest of the Chevy trucks, usually having more bells and whistles. The older truck were pretty simple and dependable. Not as much to go wrong. Power locks and windows are nice though. It's a long reach to the passenger side door.<P>3) The four door crew cab trucks are extremely long. My parents have one for pulling their RV. You can't park it in a Wal-mart parking space with other cars on either side without jocking it back and forth a few times. Those things are longer than my Suburban. A pain to drive in town, ok on the highway.<P>4) I like the extended cab trucks with the short bed. This works out to be fairly close to the same wheelbase as a regular cab long bed truck. I don't believe they made Chevy extended cab pickups in the era you're interested in. Certainly, the 3 and 4 door extended cab trucks are strictly a '90's thing.<P>5) A 3/4 ton four wheel drive pickup with straight axles and leaf springs, then lifted with big tires... rides like a damn lumber wagon to be honest. High center of gravity does not make for great handling. Gas mileage sucks.<P>6) Trucks are designed to haul a load, so when they're empty, they're light in the rear. When it's slippery out, trucks can be bad news. They often slide around pretty easily... until you lock in the front axles and shift to 4WD. Now you have the weight of the engine giving the front tires lots of bite. Since the front pulls and the rear pushes, as long as you don't go too fast, you can get through some very sloppy snow and mud. Don't ever run straight axle 4X4's in 4WD on dry hard surfaces though. The front axle needs a little slippage to work properly. Also, always use the same size tires all the way around.<P>7) Don't use kitty litter if it's ever going to get wet. It's clay and will swell and turn into mud. In the snow belt, some guys use sand bags for weight because if they get stuck in the snow or ice, sometimes the sand shoveled in front of the tires helps get you out. Remember, loose things in a pickup bed tend to slide forword each time you brake. You want the weight over the rear axles, so you might have to secure the weight in some manner. Hope this helps.||||reg||1
Z||000017||BN Boomer||10-23-2000||10:56 PM||||I think the 73 - 87 GM trucks are not as prevalent as the 88 and later models because the latter models were made in greater numbers, the higher rate of attrition of the older trucks due to their more rugged nature and higher likelihood of being offroaded harshly, and I honestly believe that people tend to hold onto the earlier models because of their aforementioned more overall ruggedness and relative ease of modification for serious offroad use.<P>"Silverado" only indicates a preferred equipment package, which generally includes such amenities as cloth seats/interior, carpeting, PW, PL, AC, fancier paint/trim, etc. In Fierospeak, it kind of like the difference between the base, SE, and GT trim levels, only without the differences in body trim. The other Chevrolet packages, "Custom Deluxe" and "Scottsdale", as well as the equivalent GMC packages, are all functionally identical, the only difference being the luxuries afforded the occupants.<P>The "extra cab" was not option on the 87-earlier models. It didn't appear until 88, only on the IFS based trucks. I think there may be some confusion here regarding "extra cabs" and "crewcabs". An extra cab is basically an additional 2-3 feet behind the seat whereas a crewcab virtually doubles the cabin space and uses full size rear doors. There was a crewcab option available on the 73-87 "C" series (2WD) and 77-91 "K" series (4WD) (the Blazers, Suburbans, and 1 ton crewcab pickups remained solid front axle based trucks until they were redesigned for the 92 model year) in both 3/4 and 1 ton platforms. Keep in mind that the crewcabs only came with fullsize 8' beds and are enormously long and correspondingly more difficult to manuever offroad.<P>You should be able to fit 31" and probably even 32" tires on a stock 73-87 GM pickup without any lift. I'm not so sure what your problem is with SUVs, but I wouldn't count on being able to "crush" them. Most of them are running those same sizes from the factory these days. Were I building up a mild offroad machine based on one of these trucks, I would probably go with a 4" lift, a 1" body lift, and some moderate fender trimming to allow the use of 35" tires. The ride will be rougher the higher you go, the brakes less effective the larger tire you run, and the steering and handling will not be at their optimum, either.||||reg||1
